From 37fe722d7b6a971481a9744c049f45283dc04494 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: StonkGuy <68372320+StonkGuy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 23:17:10 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] SMC: restart a faulting store only when it can reach the
 running block

Under SMCChecks=mtrack a guest store to a page with translated code faults.
HandleSegfault invalidates the page and, when the store could hit the block
that is executing, re-runs the storing instruction as a single-instruction
block so that a change to the block's own code is picked up at once.

The restart starts from the host state at the fault (SpillSRA), which is in the
middle of the instruction when the instruction updates registers or flags
before its store: for adc/rcl/rcr to memory NZCV already holds the new CF, a
pop to memory has already moved RSP, an x87 store-and-pop has already moved
TOP. The re-run then consumes the CF it produced or pops a second time. The
check used the whole page of the fault, so every store to data next to the
running code took this path, although such a store cannot change the block's
code.

The check now uses a window of 64 bytes on either side of the faulting address
(the largest single guest store FEX emits). Stores into the block, or close
enough to reach it, still restart as before; stores to data elsewhere on the
page return to the store, which completes now that the page is writable, and
the block runs on (its translation is already invalidated, so the next entry
translates the page again). The common path is unchanged: this only runs in
the SIGSEGV handler of an SMC write fault.

Consumer: Hell Let Loose's EAC client decrypts itself and keeps its keys and
counters on the pages of the code that uses them. 20 to 30 of these writes per
client init took the restart; one of them left a wrong value, the client called
memset on a bad address and the launcher stopped with 210 (#2). With the
narrow check it reaches 301 and plays online (reported in StonkGuy/FEX#35,
which also found the root cause).

Limits: a store that does hit the running block still restarts from the
mid-instruction state (no consumer known). An instruction with many stores
(REP MOVS/STOS, FXSAVE, XSAVE) whose first faulting store is more than 64 bytes
from the block and that later writes into the block's code runs the rest of
the block's old translation once; the next entry runs the new code.

Test: eac-tests/smc-restart-state. Before: adc, rcl, rcr, pop and fstp give
wrong results (31-32 of 1000 with the default, every iteration with
FEX_SMCHOTPAGEFAULTS=0); after: PASS in both modes, as natively. smc-same-page
unchanged (PASS), ASM 7551/7551, InstCountCI 108/108.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SyscallsSMCTracking.cpp | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SyscallsSMCTracking.cpp b/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SyscallsSMCTracking.cpp
index dbbf279..6d1b97a 100644
--- a/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SyscallsSMCTracking.cpp
+++ b/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SyscallsSMCTracking.cpp
@@ -135,9 +135,16 @@ bool SyscallHandler::HandleSegfault(FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState* Thread,
 
     FEXCORE_PROFILE_INSTANT_INCREMENT(Thread, AccumulatedSMCCount, 1);
 
+    // The restart below re-runs the faulting instruction from the host state at the fault, which is in the middle of
+    // the instruction: its flags, RSP/RSI/RDI or the x87 top may already be updated. So it is only taken when the
+    // store can reach the executing block's code, not for data that merely shares a page with it (self-decrypting code
+    // keeps its keys and counters there). A single guest store writes at most 64 bytes around the faulting address.
+    constexpr uint64_t MaxStoreSize = 64;
+    const uint64_t StoreWindowStart = FaultAddress > MaxStoreSize ? FaultAddress - MaxStoreSize : 0;
+
     auto CTX = Thread->CTX;
     if (CTX->IsAddressInCodeBuffer(Thread, ArchHelpers::Context::GetPc(ucontext)) && !CTX->IsCurrentBlockSingleInst(Thread) &&
-        CTX->IsAddressInCurrentBlock(Thread, FaultAddress & FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_MASK, FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_SIZE)) {
+        CTX->IsAddressInCurrentBlock(Thread, StoreWindowStart, 2 * MaxStoreSize)) {
       // If we are not in a single-instruction block, and the SMC write address could intersect with the current block,
       // reconstruct the context and repeat the faulting instruction as a single-instruction block so any SMC it performs
       // is immediately picked up.
